\newlabel{sec:toeplitz}{{2.3}{3}{The LTI restriction is the geometric Toeplitz kernel}{subsection.2.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The linear-SSM family reduces to one affine prefix-scan operator. \textbf  {(A)}~Each family member's parallel $\otimes $-scan reproduces its sequential recurrence to $\sim \!10^{-15}$ in \texttt  {float64}; the dashed line is the \texttt  {float64} machine epsilon. \textbf  {(B)}~The constant-gate restriction of the selective operator \emph  {is} the geometric Toeplitz kernel ($3.55\times 10^{-15}$ at $d{=}512$, flat in width), while a genuinely selective control cannot be reproduced by any single geometric kernel ($4.9\times 10^{-2}$) --- a thirteen-order-of-magnitude separation.}}{4}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kernel}{{1}{4}{The linear-SSM family reduces to one affine prefix-scan operator. \textbf {(A)}~Each family member's parallel $\otimes $-scan reproduces its sequential recurrence to $\sim \!10^{-15}$ in \code {float64}; the dashed line is the \code {float64} machine epsilon. \textbf {(B)}~The constant-gate restriction of the selective operator \emph {is} the geometric Toeplitz kernel ($3.55\times 10^{-15}$ at $d{=}512$, flat in width), while a genuinely selective control cannot be reproduced by any single geometric kernel ($4.9\times 10^{-2}$) --- a thirteen-order-of-magnitude separation}{figure.caption.4}{}}
